## Standing methodology (hard-won, do not skip)
1. Grade on how turns **ENDED** (`evals/armstats.py`). Prefer **VERIFIED**.
   NEVER `clean-finish` (counts surrender as success). `DONE` alone is too
   generous: b94 r7 declared completion with the suite red.
2. **Check exposure before reading any table**, matching the trigger **both
   arms emit**, never the new wording.
3. **Open a winning run and read the trajectory** before crediting anything.
   Worth more than the score table three separate times now.
4. Probe the mlx server with a real 2-token completion, not `/v1/models`.
5. Anchor any log watch to a byte offset captured at launch.
6. **Calibrate before crediting.** A p-value is not a noise floor.
7. **Measure before prioritising, even your own alarms.**
8. **Grade a message change on its target event rate AND on the endings.** b97
   won its own metric outright (landing 47%→72%, not-found 7→0) while every run
   died (VERIFIED 5/8→0/8).
9. **The route named first is the route taken** (5.20b). Order by authoring
   burden: the route that makes the model write least goes first.
10. **Two identical degenerate arms mean the HARNESS, not the change.**
11. **A sweep that exits is not a sweep that ran.** Read the log tail.
12. **When a lever's exposure has collapsed, say so and change the grading
    method** — don't run the sweep anyway and read the noise.
13. **An underpowered test does not return "no effect", it returns nothing** —
    and the two must never be printed with the same word (5.27).
14. **Print what the model actually saw.** Build 103's defect passed every unit
    test; it was only visible in the rendered string on a live run.
15. **Rank levers by what FOLLOWS the message, not by how often it fires**
    (5.28). The ambiguous message was queued as the top remaining target on 20
    events; measured, 32 of 42 are answered with a successful `replace_lines`,
    0 re-send, 0 syntax errors. Exposure says where to look; the next action
    says whether there is anything to fix.
16. **Replay the fix against the archive before spending GPU on it** (5.29).
    `$CLAUDE_JOB_DIR/tmp/replay106b.py` re-ran every stored syntax rejection
    through the new `try_edit` in ten minutes: 87 rescued of 88 judgeable. It
    also found both of the draft's defects. Two traps it walked into first:
    the event log **clips tool result content**, so file state must be rebuilt
    from `evals/cases/<case>/seed` forward, not from `read_file`; and the
    broken version reported "no reach in the current sweeps" rather than
    "cannot tell" (methodology 13 in a new place). And a replay measures the
    population it drew from, not the one the next sweep produces — 5.30.
17. **A lever that fires zero times has not been tested** (5.30). b106-indent
    reported a difference on every summary line and the rescue had never run
    once; the whole table was arm noise between identical builds. Count the
    firings BEFORE reading any metric — which is why the rescue announces
    itself in the result text.
19. **A steer that asks for narration will be answered with narration** (5.32).
    Every nudge must name a tool and demand a call. Grade the whole set on
    prose-only share (`afterturn.py`); 3 of 11 are above 25%.
20. **Count the target event in the CURRENT base arm before building the fix**
    (5.31). Three sweeps went into an indentation bug whose event rate had
    already fallen from 2.31/run to 0.04/run, because an earlier change routed
    models around it. The archive said the bug was everywhere; the archive was
    describing a population that no longer exists.
21. **Split a per-event rate by arm on a sweep whose arms were identical, and
    read the spread as the noise floor** (5.32). b107-indent's arms were
    behaviourally the same (rescue fired once in 14 runs), yet prose-only after
    the level-1 same-failure nudge read 56% base vs 76% cand — a ±20pt band at
    n≈25. Build 108 must land under ~40%, judged against ITS OWN base arm.
18. **The exact tier is not exact.** `text.count(old)` is a SUBSTRING test, so
    a dedented single-line `old` matches mid-line and `str.replace` splices
    there. Any reasoning that starts "an exact match means `old` was
    reproduced byte for byte" is wrong.
22. **Fix the first steer in a cascade, not the loudest one.** The loudest
    nudges in the b107 census were symptoms of a run that had already gone
    wrong two nudges earlier; converting the first one took the rest with it
    (nudges/run 9.4 -> 3.8).
23. **An A/A does not just size the noise — it re-reads your headline number.**
    `aa14-calib` was launched to calibrate the score and did. What it actually
    corrected was the mechanism metric the b108 verdict was built on (VERIFIED
    7/14, which identical code reproduces at 3 and 4), because nobody thinks to
    question the number that moved the furthest. Run the A/A against **every**
    channel you credited, not just the one you distrusted.
25. **Build the grader against the OLD sweep before the new one lands** and
    check it reproduces the number in the write-up. Two of the last three
    metrics specified in advance failed that check — b110's "re-applied the
    same text" (1 hit in 20 events; the re-apply is a VARIANT, not a byte
    copy) and `_nudge_bucket`'s malformed histogram — and both would otherwise
    have surfaced only after 2.3h of GPU, as arms that disagree by nothing
    because the ruler is wrong.
24. **When a rate is measured before and after a fix upstream of it, the
    population changed.** The escalated nudges read 18%/4% prose-only pre-108
    and 65%/74% after. Nothing about them regressed — build 108 removed the
    easy runs from their input. A before/after on a downstream metric is not a
    comparison unless the funnel above it held still.

27. **A per-nudge rate is a per-RUN draw, and it decays with depth.** The
    events cluster: runs come out 0/2 or 2/2, so "n=22 events" is n=11 draws
    and every p-value quoted on an event count is inflated. And prose after a
    steer runs 4% (iter 9-16) -> 32% (17-24) -> 80% (25+), while the cand arm
    fires ~11 iterations deeper than base — which is the ENTIRE base-vs-cand
    asymmetry seen on identical code in aa14 and b110. Cluster by run before
    quoting n; check firing depth before crediting an arm difference.
    **Except:** the 5.32 recipe is 0 prose in 76 events at EVERY depth. Depth
    predicts narration only for steers that don't name the call. ROADMAP 5.37.

## Mined and DECLINED at level 1 (5.39) — do not rebuild
- **"Prescribe the whole sequence in the level-1 note."** Survivors go
  read -> edit -> bash 14/14; half the dead detour via `update_plan`. Looked
  decisive until I followed the dead branch two calls further: **all 27
  detours reach bash anyway.** Both groups run the same loop; the difference
  is whether the edit was RIGHT. Capability wall, not a steering one.
- **"Dying runs don't test their edits."** Pooled: 75% vs 57%. Per-run median:
  60% vs 67% — the gap REVERSES. Clustering trap (methodology 27); dying runs
  have more edits so pooling weights them by the thing that kills them.

28. **Never bundle a behaviour change into a sweep that tests WORDING.** The
    temptation is that the behaviour change is obviously correct and
    unrelated. 5.36's was neither, and one bundled line cost the attribution
    of a 2.4h sweep.
29. **Before "fixing" a metric in the product, check where the metric is
    computed.** 5.36's lived in `armstats.py` the whole time. The fix there
    cost nothing and risked nothing; in `loop.py` it changed the agent to
    correct a number the agent does not produce.

30. **A mechanism check must pin EVERY dimension the claim names.** "The edit
    targeted text outside the window" is a claim about one file; the check
    allowed any file and so counted the most ordinary thing the model does
    (read the test, edit the source) as the pathology. And one confirming
    trajectory can illustrate an aggregate, never test it — reading r10 is
    what made the wrong number feel verified. Re-derive a decisive-looking
    census with the tightest predicate BEFORE spending a sweep on it.
31. **A steer that converts but changes no outcome is asking for a STOP, not
    better wording.** Three sweeps went into making the escalated same-failure
    steer land (5.32 -> 5.40); it ended at 100% compliance and 1 verified
    finish in 69. The compliance channel measures whether the model DID the
    thing; it says nothing about whether the thing was worth doing. Once
    conversion is maxed and the outcome is flat, the honest read is that the
    population is dead and the lever is a budget, not a phrasing. Ask "what
    does the turn spend after this fires?" before writing another version of
    it.
32. **When two guards watch the same failure, prefer the one keyed on
    IDENTITY over the one keyed on TEXT.** `max_error_stall` keys on the error
    string and fires ~10 iterations late because the model rewords what it
    gets wrong; the same-failure counter keys on which test is failing and
    tracks it exactly. The stop belongs on the signal that is right, not on
    the one that happens to already have a stop.
33. **A lever that never fires turns your A/B into an A/A — read it as one.**
    b115's cap fired 0 times, so both arms ran identical code. That sweep is
    not wasted: it is a free calibration of the instrument, and it produced the
    best number in the project (5.45). Before filing a null result, check
    whether the arms were actually DIFFERENT; if they were not, grade the
    instrument with the data you just bought.
34. **A change you cannot see fire cannot be graded — put the transition on the
    wire in the SAME build that adds it.** Build 115 shipped with no event for
    arming its own budget, so "never armed" and "armed, never expired" were
    indistinguishable in the archive and the sweep could not answer its own
    question. Methodology 2, learned again, cheaply.

35. **An A/B pins the code; only the PAIRED design pins the machine.** Before
    comparing an absolute rate across two sweeps, prove they ran against the
    same server process. A restart of `:8081` — same model, same weights, same
    alias, same temperature — moved clean finishes from 0-7/14 to 4-11/14 and
    collapsed the model's plan from 3 variants to 1 across 140 runs. It cost me
    a headline I had already emailed (5.47). Build 117 stamps pid + start time
    into every `ab.json`; check it before any cross-sweep sentence.

36. **When an early branch decides the run, report the BRANCH MIX beside the
    metric.** In b110-b116 the 3rd tool call splits runs 78% vs 3% on VERIFIED
    from a byte-identical prior context, and the per-arm draw of that branch
    explains b116's 4/14-vs-11/14 better than the lever under test does
    (5.48). A lever downstream of a decisive coin flip cannot be graded by a
    metric the coin flip dominates.
37. **Verify the event schema before writing an extractor over it.** Bash args
    are keyed `cmd`, not `command`. My extractor silently returned "" for every
    run and I reported "36/36 read-first runs never ran pytest" — 100%
    confident, completely false; both arms run pytest, the losing one more
    often. A predicate that returns the same answer for every row in the
    population is a bug until proven otherwise (5.52).
38. **Grade a steer by what it converts TO, not whether it converts.** The
    multi-match message converts at 76/76 and is the losing branch's message:
    what it prescribes ("pass only the replacement line as `new`") is the
    losing strategy. Sharpens rule 31 — a perfectly-converting steer can be
    worse than neutral (5.52).
39. **When the case's bug shapes align with a strategy, "strategy X loses" may
    just be "this case punishes X."** exec-bugfix has 2 one-line bugs and 1
    needing a block re-derivation, so it rewards block rewriting by
    construction. State the confound before generalizing off a single case.

40. Pilot a new instrument for whether the LEVER FIRES, not whether the
    metric moves. exec-ambig v1 fired 0 ambiguous messages in a paired pilot:
    I duplicated the wrong LINE, but the model always sends the enclosing
    guard as `old`, which was unique. Ambiguity is a property of the string
    the model actually sends. ~10 min saved a 2.5h sweep whose result would
    have read as 'the message doesn't help'. (ROADMAP 5.56)

41. A metric read from the model's own self-check measures WHEN the arm last
    looked, not what it left behind. b121's secondary said the candidate was
    0.36 tests worse; runs editing after their final pytest were cand 14/14 vs
    base 0/14, so it scored one arm mid-repair and the other at rest. Check the
    read point is arm-independent before differencing. Sibling of 37.
    (ROADMAP 5.57)

42. An A/A can STRENGTHEN a result, not only kill one. b121's A/A found a ~2x
    arm-slot bias favouring BASE (identical code: 12/24 base vs 6/24 cand), the
    slot build 119 was never in — so the pre-registered p was conservative, and
    builds could then be compared WITHIN one slot (119: 12/24 vs 118: 0/38,
    p=1.25e-06), removing the bias entirely. Run calibration before deciding
    what it would have meant. (ROADMAP 5.59)

43. WRITE THE DENOMINATOR DOWN NEXT TO THE NUMERATOR, or you will pair them
    wrong. Third instance now: `cmd`/`command` (37), `rep`/`repeat` (grade121),
    and lever 0b, whose whole premise was "96/96 and 78/132" — actually 48/96
    and 36/132, i.e. I divided a TWO-arm no-op count by a ONE-arm edit count and
    read a per-arm edit count as a no-op count. Consequence: the bar I
    pre-registered (cand "under a third") was already met by the BASELINE, so
    the sweep would have credited the build for nothing. The pattern is always
    the same — a number quoted from memory in prose instead of recomputed by the
    grader. Every rate in a pre-registration must come out of the same script
    that will grade it, run against a CLOSED sweep first. (ROADMAP 5.65)

44. A pre-registration may be AMENDED, never quietly. The 5.65 amendment is
    legitimate because the correction came from closed sweeps (b121-aa, b122)
    while b124 was still on its base arm, and it is written in the document
    above the result with the old numbers left visible. Fixing a bar after
    seeing the candidate's data is a different act entirely.

45. WHEN A MESSAGE MAKES TWO DEMANDS, THIS MODEL OBEYS THE FIRST AND DROPS THE
    SECOND — and re-ranking them just swaps which one it drops. Proven both
    ways at n=24: build 119 (copy first) got 29% no-ops, build 121 (correct
    first) got 100% fragments. Both significant, both useless. So a wording
    that isn't working is not a prompt to reorder the clauses; the next move is
    to DELETE a demand, usually by giving the model a mechanism that makes the
    demand unnecessary. Build 123's `occurrence` is that move. (ROADMAP 5.66)

46. BEFORE BUILDING ON A RECOMMENDATION IN MY OWN ROADMAP, RE-DERIVE ITS
    PREMISE FROM THE ARCHIVE. 5.66 recommended auto-widening in prose that
    sounded airtight; ten minutes of counting showed 0 of 245 cases were
    widenable, for a reason that is obvious once stated (a context window drawn
    around a match always contains the match). Prose reasoning about a corpus
    is not evidence about that corpus, even when I wrote the prose.

47. THE TOOL DESCRIPTION MAY BE A STRONGER LEVER THAN THE ERROR MESSAGE, and I
    spent six builds on the wrong one. Builds 119/121/123 all rewrote the
    ambiguous-match MESSAGE, which the model reads only after it is already
    stuck. Build 123 incidentally added one sentence to `edit_file`'s
    DESCRIPTION, which it reads before it does anything, and the case went
    7/24 -> 22/24 by changing where the model AIMED — 21/24 runs never saw the
    message at all. **CONFIRMED by b126**: removing that one sentence and
    keeping everything else took fixed 18/24 -> 7/24 (p=0.0034) and wide-aim
    15/24 -> 0/24. Two replications per condition, opposite arm slots. Still
    one tool, one case: the generalisation to check next, on a DIFFERENT tool
    and a DIFFERENT case, is that when a tool fails the same way over and over,
    fix the description, not the failure message. (ROADMAP 5.69/5.71)

48. EVERY PRE-REGISTRATION MUST CARRY ONE POPULATION-INDEPENDENT OUTCOME. 5.67
    scoped all five of its calls to "edits that follow an ambiguous message",
    because that was the failure I knew about — and the fix arrived upstream by
    stopping the ambiguous message from happening, which made four of the five
    calls unreadable. `fully_fixed` was in there as call 5 and is the only
    reason the sweep could be graded at all. A metric conditioned on the
    failure you are fixing goes blind exactly when you succeed. (5.69; the
    fourth relative of rule 43.)

49. AN ASSOCIATION POOLED ACROSS THE ARCHIVE IS CONFOUNDED BY BUILD — STRATIFY
    BY LABEL BEFORE BELIEVING IT. Picking the transfer target, I found that
    exec-bugfix runs hitting a `replace_lines` syntax reject were fixed 51% vs
    4% and nearly built a lever on it. Stratifying by label killed it: the
    reject-heavy runs cluster in b110-b120, whose fix rates are 54-71% for
    unrelated reasons. The archive spans ~40 builds; ANY behaviour that became
    more common over time correlates with every improvement shipped since.
    Compute the within-label breakdown and a pooled OR, and print the strata —
    if one era carries the effect, there is no effect. (ROADMAP 5.73)

50. A MARKER THAT CO-OCCURS WITH SUCCESS MAY BE INCIDENTAL TO THE WINNING
    TRAJECTORY, NOT CAUSAL — AND TWO TRAJECTORIES SETTLE IN MINUTES WHAT
    STATISTICS ARGUE ABOUT FOR AN HOUR. Even stratified, the reject signal
    looked real (0/132 clean-use runs were EVER fixed, in every stratum).
    Reading one winning and one losing run explained it instantly: the rejects
    are the two failed detours a winning run makes on its way BACK to wide
    `edit_file` calls. Suppressing them would not have passed a single run. The
    causal variable was where the model aimed, which was visible in the first
    line of both trajectories. Third time this rule has paid: see the
    `read-a-winning-run-before-crediting-it` note. (ROADMAP 5.73)

51. AUDIT MODEL-FACING TEXT VIA THE RUNTIME STRING, NOT THE SOURCE. Every tool
    description in this repo is a parenthesised concatenation split across
    source lines, so `grep` for any phrase spanning a line break finds NOTHING.
    Three of 5.77's defects were invisible that way, including `edit_file`
    asserting "must match once unless replace_all is true" — false since build
    123. Import the class and search `Tool.description`; and check WHERE in the
    string a claim sits, because position demonstrably matters (the falsehood
    is at 23%, its correction at 56%). (ROADMAP 5.77)

52. PRIORITISE INSTRUCTION FIXES BY EXPOSURE x EVIDENCE, NOT BY HOW BAD THEY
    READ. The worst-reading defect in 5.77 (the truncation nudge still shipping
    a deleted clause) fires in 2.7% of runs; the highest-value ones are in tool
    descriptions, which 100% of runs read and which b126 proved are the
    strongest surface in the product. Count nudge frequency across the archive
    before ranking anything. And separate FALSEHOODS from OMISSIONS — but
    **AMENDED 2026-08-11**: the original wording said correcting a falsehood is
    "not a behavioural bet and can ship together". b128 falsified that. A TRUE
    statement can do harm: what the model does with a sentence is independent
    of its truth value. Correct falsehoods, but in a high-exposure string
    (anything 100% of runs read) give the correction its own build and its own
    A/B. Ship several corrections together only where exposure is measurably
    ZERO on the graded cases — as D2 was.

53. A GUARD THAT VOIDS AN EXPERIMENT MUST BE ARM-DIRECTIONAL. b128's VOID fired
    on "false completions nonzero in ANY arm", and 5 of the 6 were in the
    UNCHANGED base arm. Candidate false completions void the CANDIDATE; base
    false completions invalidate the BASELINE and are their own bug report.
    Reading the base's number as candidate misconduct is backwards, and
    dismissing it as noise would have missed a real correctness bug.

54. WHEN A PRE-REGISTERED RULE FIRES, EXECUTE IT, THEN EXPLAIN. b128 was
    rejected on the numbers before a single trajectory was opened; the
    trajectories were read only to explain the loss. 5.76 said in advance "no
    re-reading of the trajectories to find a reason to keep it", and the value
    of saying that in advance is entirely in honouring it afterwards.

56. A PER-RUN STATISTIC EXPRESSED AS A FRACTION OF THE RUN IS RETROSPECTIVE.
    "The nudge fires at 0.94 of the run" divides by the FINAL length, which no
    running loop knows — it cannot drive a runtime decision. Restate in
    absolute iterations before designing against it. Corollary: before
    crediting a marker, bin by how many OPPORTUNITIES the run had to exhibit
    it. "Never repeated a call" and "barely ran" are the same population until
    shown otherwise, and that mistake inflated lever 0e from 8/8 to 54/54.

55. NEVER LET HARNESS TEXT BECOME A MODEL UTTERANCE. Anything written into the
    ASSISTANT role — elision markers, placeholders, summaries — is training the
    next token. The model will copy it, and a copy with no tool call reads as a
    finished turn. Rewording is not a fix; guard the echo. Check any new
    assistant-role injection against this before shipping it.

57. CALIBRATE BEFORE YOU THRESHOLD. A decision rule on a metric whose A/A spread
    you have not measured is numerology, not pre-registration. Read the noise
    floor at the TOP of this file first, then set the line outside it. b121-aa
    existed and would have voided 5.81's rule before it was written.

58. NEVER GATE ON CROSS-SWEEP REPRODUCTION OF A PER-RUN METRIC. Sweeps are
    paired within themselves for a reason. A sanity check must be either a
    within-sweep quantity or a per-call rate with n in the hundreds. 5.87's gate
    is "base replace_lines calls <= 10" — within-sweep, per-call, and it needs
    no earlier sweep's score to interpret.

59. AN UNREAD RESULT IS WORSE THAN NO RESULT. It costs the compute AND buys
    false confidence. When a sweep finishes, read it against every OPEN
    question, not just the one it was launched for.

60. WHEN AN AUDIT'S ANSWER IS "CHANGE NOTHING", SHIP THAT ANSWER. After finding
    a lever the pull is to justify the search by rewriting text on the theory
    alone. D2 and b126 were both exactly that, and both lost. A prohibition with
    no measurable signature stays. (5.88 cleared 7 of 8 negated clauses.)

